    "b-sks-rc-patterns-resolution-heading": {
      "id": "b-sks-rc-patterns-resolution-heading",
      "type": "heading",
      "props": {
        "level": 2
      },
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "Resolution Moves Are Patterns"
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-sks-rc-patterns-resolution-body": {
      "id": "b-sks-rc-patterns-resolution-body",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {},
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "A resolution move describes how \"mismatches of shape X resolve by Y\". It is not its own table. It is a "
        },
        {
          "insert": "pattern",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " entity whose substance is an ordinary fact: a "
        },
        {
          "insert": "value",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " stating the move, a "
        },
        {
          "insert": "rationale",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " arguing for it, a "
        },
        {
          "insert": "confidence",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": ", and "
        },
        {
          "insert": "evidence",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " rows pointing at the worker-run submissions where the move worked ("
        },
        {
          "insert": "attempt://",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " locators). Example fact: \"two stores to the same struct swapped in the diff resolve by swapping the source assignments\", its evidence pinning the submissions whose scores rose after the swap."
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-sks-rc-patterns-resolution-body-continued": {
      "id": "b-sks-rc-patterns-resolution-body-continued",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {},
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "Formerly this knowledge lived in the retired "
        },
        {
          "insert": "tactic",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": ", "
        },
        {
          "insert": "tactic_use",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": ", and "
        },
        {
          "insert": "pattern_tactic",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " tables. The raw submission descriptions and hypotheses stay in the ledger, so structure can be rebuilt from that data later if a real population emerges."
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-sks-rc-patterns-consulted-heading": {
      "id": "b-sks-rc-patterns-consulted-heading",
      "type": "heading",
      "props": {
        "level": 2
      },
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "Consulted, Not Matched"
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-sks-rc-patterns-consulted-body": {
      "id": "b-sks-rc-patterns-consulted-body",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {},
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "Patterns are not lookups, and there is no recognizer machinery: nothing fires them, ranks them, or turns them into a checklist. The librarian reads patterns while indexing new material, judges whether it shows a known convention, and cites a pattern only when it thinks the connection is real. Workers see them on the target card as information with provenance, never as a checklist or ranked prescriptions. Builder pipelines may propose "
        },
        {
          "insert": "pattern",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " entities; the librarian admits and writes."
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-sks-rc-patterns-admission-heading": {
      "id": "b-sks-rc-patterns-admission-heading",
      "type": "heading",
      "props": {
        "level": 2
      },
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "Admission"
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-sks-rc-patterns-admission-body": {
      "id": "b-sks-rc-patterns-admission-body",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {},
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "The librarian writes the rows through the standard curated admission path, with one extra actor: the mechanical builders ("
        },
        {
          "insert": "mismatch-patterns",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": ", "
        },
        {
          "insert": "opseq-similarity",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": ") propose, and only propose. A builder's output never becomes an "
        },
        {
          "insert": "entity",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " row directly. Discord material enters the system the same way: the importer lands it as facts and evidence on knowledge subjects, mostly patterns and game concepts, never as ledger entries."
        }
      ],
      "children": []
    },
    "b-sks-rc-patterns-admission-process": {
      "id": "b-sks-rc-patterns-admission-process",
      "type": "process-outline",
      "props": {
        "steps": [
          {
            "text": "Admit a pattern",
            "steps": [
              {
                "text": "A builder or a human notices a recurring convention and proposes a `pattern` entity: a draft locator, draft facts, candidate links."
              },
              {
                "text": "The librarian judges the proposal: admit it as a new entity, fold it into an existing one, or discard it."
              },
              {
                "text": "On admission, substance lands as ordinary facts (typically `purpose`) citing `discord://` and `code://` evidence; reach lands as `link` rows, each with a `why` and an inline `kind` + `locator` citation."
              },
              {
                "text": "Game concepts follow the same curated path, usually seeded from the wiki importer. See the Game Concepts sibling page.",
                "kind": "note"
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "children": []
    },
